  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Definition of the Unit Impulse",
    "lesson_core_move": "Characterize the unit impulse by using its zero-width, unit-area behavior to treat $\\delta$(t) as the impulse building block for continuous-time signal descriptions.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Definition of the Unit Impulse",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Definition of the Unit Impulse",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Ordinary signals are described by finite widths and finite heights, but the impulse is introduced as a limiting point-concentrated signal. You should recognize the core object first: $\\delta(t)$ is defined by a zero-width support idea, not by a usual shape you can inspect point by point.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Your first action is to treat support as the object criterion: the unit impulse is concentrated at $t=0$, so it is identified by where it acts in the continuum, not by an ordinary pointwise value. Keep in mind that practical use is tied to what happens under integration.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this pair of properties together as the operating definition: for $t\\neq 0$, $\\delta(t)=0$, and $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t)\\,dt=1$. The second part fixes the impulse's meaning as a unit-area building block.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case model to check intuition. As $\\epsilon \\to 0^+$, write $\\delta_\\epsilon(t)=\\frac{1}{\\epsilon}[u(t+\\epsilon/2)-u(t-\\epsilon/2)]$. The pulse gets narrower and taller while preserving unit area, which is exactly the limiting behavior you will reuse in later sections.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t)\\,dt=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t)=\\lim_{\\epsilon\\to 0^+}\\frac{1}{\\epsilon}\\,[u(t+\\epsilon/2)-u(t-\\epsilon/2)]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Definition of the Unit Impulse",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing $\\delta$(t) as a Point Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Identify the point-support interpretation of $f(t)=\\delta(t)$: choose the correct statement about where the signal acts and what quantity it contributes under integration.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: the expression is written as $\\delta(t)$, so you should treat it as a point-support object, not a finite-width pulse. The support is therefore $\\{0\\}$, meaning the signal is concentrated at the single instant $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the rule from the prior definition: $\\delta(t)=0$ for all $t\\neq 0$, and its identifying quantity is area, not pointwise value. So for a unit impulse at the origin the unit-area condition is $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t)\\,dt=1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Execute the matching step. Since no offset appears, $\\delta(t)$ is exactly the same as $\\delta(t-0)$, so the support point and the unit-area interpretation are unchanged. That is the key recognition move you will reuse when you evaluate options like $\\delta(t-a)$ or combinations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t)=\\delta(t-0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: the correct statement is that $f(t)=\\delta(t)$ contributes one unit of area at $t=0$, so it should be interpreted by support and integral action, not as an ordinary pointwise value at zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Definition of the Unit Impulse",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Identifying the Zero-Width Behavior of $\\delta$(t)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate the limit model $x_\\epsilon(t)=\\frac{1}{\\epsilon}\\,\\mathbf{1}_{|t|<\\epsilon/2}$ and state the zero-width implication for $t\\ne 0$ and the preserved area property.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: this step adds one controlled wrinkle beyond pure support recognition by introducing a width-to-zero family. Start from the interval form, identify that for each fixed $\\epsilon>0$, the pulse is nonzero only on $-\\epsilon/2 < t < \\epsilon/2$ and has height $1/\\epsilon$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compute the defining invariant first. For generic $\\epsilon$, integrate directly over the active interval: $\\int_{-\\epsilon/2}^{\\epsilon/2} \\frac{1}{\\epsilon}\\,dt = 1$, so the area is fixed at one before taking any limit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\epsilon/2}^{\\epsilon/2}\\frac{1}{\\epsilon}\\,dt = 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now track value and area separately as $\\epsilon\\to 0^+$. For $t\\ne 0$, the interval eventually excludes $t$, so $\\lim_{\\epsilon\\to 0^+}x_\\epsilon(t)=0$. At the same time, the unit-area identity remains intact, which is the signature of zero-width concentration.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\lim_{\\epsilon\\to 0^+}x_\\epsilon(t)=0 \\text{ for } t\\neq 0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: this model shrinks toward a point-support signal with support at $t=0$ while preserving unit area, so it behaves like the impulse abstraction used in this lesson's core move.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Definition of the Unit Impulse",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting the Unit Area of $\\delta$(t)",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Compute the area of $\\delta(t)$ and $3\\delta(t)$ under the same integral convention, and interpret what the scalar does.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: this step keeps the zero-width support fixed at $t=0$ and adds one controlled wrinkle: coefficient scaling. The task is to interpret the scalar in front of the impulse as a signal area weight.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Execute step one. Use the base rule for the unit impulse: for the unscaled atom, the area is $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t)\\,dt=1$. This is the same baseline used in earlier steps.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t)\\,dt=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Execute step two with linearity. Since $x(t)=3\\delta(t)$, $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)\\,dt=3\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t)\\,dt=3\\cdot 1=3$, so the area scales by the same coefficient.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}3\\delta(t)\\,dt=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: $3\\delta(t)$ has unit-support at $t=0$ but area $3$, so it is not interpreted as a pointwise peak value; it is interpreted as a weighted impulse event.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Definition of the Unit Impulse",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Using $\\delta$(t) as a Continuous-Time Signal Building Block",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write a signal description for two impulse events at the origin: one unit impulse and one weighted by $2$, then simplify it into a single weighted impulse expression.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: both events are at the same support point $t=0$, so this step keeps the same location rule from prior sections and adds only one wrinkle: combining coefficients at matching support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Translate the verbal events into symbols using the same rule: one unit event is $\\delta(t)$, and the weighted event is $2\\delta(t)$. So the signal is $x(t)=\\delta(t)+2\\delta(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply additivity on matching support points. Since both terms share support $t=0$, combine their scalar coefficients: $\\delta(t)+2\\delta(t)=3\\delta(t)$. This is the core composition move for impulse-based representations.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\delta(t)+2\\delta(t)=3\\delta(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: the simplified representation is a single weighted impulse at $t=0$ with coefficient $3$, which means area $3$ at the same instant and no change in support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
